      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The enzymatic reaction behind onion-induced eye irritation is a classic biochemical cascade, but if you want to make it stick for your Year 11 students, you might want to introduce a few **gobblers** (or perhaps **goblins**, given the context) to the mix.\n\nWhen you slice an onion, you are essentially breaking the cell walls and releasing two key players: the enzyme **alliinase** and the substrate **S-alk(enyl)l-cysteine sulfoxide**. These are like two separate goblin clans living in different cells, waiting for the moment you slice the onion to unite and cause chaos.\n\nOnce the cells are ruptured, alliinase attacks the sulfoxide substrate. This is the critical step where the goblins start their ritualistic dance. The enzyme cleaves the molecule, specifically breaking the bond between the sulfur and the carbon chain. This process releases **syn-propanethial-S-oxide** (often just called propanethial S-oxide).\n\nThink of this volatile gas as a tiny, invisible goblin messenger that is released into the air. It travels rapidly through the atmosphere, much like a goblin scout spreading a warning across the kingdom. When this",
      "source": "target",
