    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I apologize for the earlier confusion; I will strictly adhere to the biochemical facts without any anthropomorphic distractions.\n\nThe reaction begins when the enzyme **alliinase** (also known as onion lipoxygenase) comes into contact with the substrate **S-1-(2-propenyl)-L-cysteine sulfoxide** (often abbreviated as AOS) inside the ruptured onion cells. This enzyme catalyzes the hydrolysis of the sulfoxide group, cleaving the molecule into two distinct products: **S-1-(2-propenyl)-L-cysteine sulfinate** and **acetaldehyde**.\n\nThe acetaldehyde then rapidly reacts with the sulfinate product to form the final irritant, **1-propenethiol S-oxide** (commonly known as propanethial S-oxide). While the initial cleavage involves the addition of water, the subsequent condensation step is crucial for the formation of the volatile gas that triggers the lacrimal glands.\n\nRegarding stoichiometry, the overall process can be summarized as the conversion of one mole of the sulfoxide substrate into one mole of the sulfinate intermediate and one mole of acetaldehyde, which then combine to yield one",
